{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flowercomputers/bogkit/pulls/5","id":4134218420,"node_id":"PR_kwDOTVpH8872ayq0","html_url":"https://github.com/flowercomputers/bogkit/pull/5","diff_url":"https://github.com/flowercomputers/bogkit/pull/5.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/flowercomputers/bogkit/pull/5.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flowercomputers/bogkit/issues/5","number":5,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"tompkins-audio-search — a searchable, multimodal memory of a park, on fold","user":{"login":"dnbrwstr","id":357216,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM1NzIxNg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/357216?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr","html_url":"https://github.com/dnbrwstr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"## Hackathon submission\n\n### category\n\npick **one**:\n\n- [ ] agent support\n- [ ] performance\n- [x] novel interface / gaming\n\n### team\n\n- **project name:** tompkins-audio-search\n- **team / author name(s):** Dan Brewster\n- **contact (optional):** dnbrwstr@gmail.com\n\n### what you built\n\nA searchable, multimodal memory of Tompkins Square Park. Ask for spoken words, weather, city sounds or bird species and land on the moment the archive remembers — on one wall-clock timeline spanning the whole corpus, not a file browser.\n\nThe interface is the timeline. Every stream in the archive is placed on a single 53.8-day axis (108 placements, 3 lanes where microphones overlap), search hits are highlighted on it, and clicking anywhere resolves to playable audio. Playback is the timeline; there is no separate player.\n\n**How it uses bog-kit.** One `fold::stream::KeyedStream<RecordKey, Record>` carries all five record types into a 12-way tuple fan-out, so a single `upsert` atomically updates the acoustic vector index, transcript BM25 postings, the transcript embedding graph, bird labels and embeddings, the per-stream time index, the species vocabulary and the coverage views — and one `remove` retracts it from all of them.\n\nThat guarantee is the reason for the design, and it's exercised on real data: dropping a 30,668-record stream cleared every index in one operation, verified from outside (species vocabulary → 0, bird and speech queries → empty, playable hours 35.83 → 23.81, no orphans).\n\n- `anny` backs three typed HNSW indexes — CLAP 512-d, `ese` 512-d, BirdNET 1024-d\n- `ese` embeds transcripts *inside* the pipeline, which retraction requires: it must be a pure function of the text so cancelling re-derives the same vector\n- `terminal::KeyedRanked` range scans are what make \"a bird while it is raining\" mean conjunction *in time* — it narrows 77 episodes to 1\n- Record keys are stable across reprocessing (`model_version` lives in the record body, not the key), so re-running a model *replaces* evidence instead of accumulating a stale copy\n\nInference never holds the write lock: Python workers stage checksummed batches and hand them to the single store owner for a short commit. `anny`'s HNSW state is `Rc<RefCell<_>>` so `Store` isn't `Send`, which forces exactly the single-owner thread `fold`'s single-writer discipline wants anyway.\n\n### how to run\n\n```bash\n# from repo root\ncargo test -p tompkins-audio-search   # 92 tests, no data or credentials needed\ncargo run -p tompkins-audio-search    # subcommands: manifest / timeline / commit / serve / forget\n```\n\n**What a judge can actually run:** the test suite, standalone. It's the honest entry point — most of the interesting behaviour is pinned there (PTS wraparound across 358 simulated hours, gap handling, atomic retraction, rank fusion, episode grouping, conjunction).\n\nThe ingestion path needs read access to a private Oda S3 bucket plus a local cross-reference catalog, so it isn't reproducible outside this machine. `examples/tompkins-audio-search/README.md` documents the full pipeline, and `docs/adr/` records the decisions.\n\n### demo / notes\n\nBuilt against ~47.6 h of real archive audio. Some findings that shaped it:\n\n- **The playlists are live sliding windows, not VOD** (~12 segments, no `ENDLIST`), so they can't enumerate a 358-hour stream. Order comes from object numbering instead.\n- **`EXTINF` isn't imprecise, it's wrong.** It advertises 93-frame segments; decoded PTS shows 91/92/93/94. Trusting it drifts **−690 s over 24 h** and −2.87 h over the full stream. Time is accumulated in 90 kHz ticks so rounding can't reintroduce it.\n- **PTS wraps every ~26.5 h**; the longest stream crosses that ~13 times. Unhandled, a late seek lands hours away.\n- **Nothing carries `EXT-X-PROGRAM-DATE-TIME`**, so no wall clock is exact. The UI says so on every view: exact within a stream, approximate between streams.\n- **CLAP zero-shot tags needed per-label calibration.** Scored by softmax, \"bicycle\" fired on 42% of windows and no window came out untagged — a per-window normalisation can't express \"none of these apply\". Calibrated against each label's own corpus distribution, the same tag lands at 0.13%.\n- **k-NN always returns k results**, so a query for something absent still filled a page. Untagged hits measured 0.005–0.043 similarity against the 0.25 a tag requires; there's now a floor, and the number is shown.\n\n**Known issue, documented rather than hidden:** large stretches of the archive are phase-inverted (L/R correlation to −0.95). The workers decoded with `-ac 1` = `(L+R)/2`, which *cancels* that — 9.3 dB lost, the speech band down from 6.6% to 0.01%. The first diagnosis was also wrong: scanning through the same downmix reported 87/96 assets as dead audio, where a single channel put it at 0/12. Fixed in this PR; the committed indexes predate the fix and need re-running.\n\nMilestone 5 (the evaluation gate) is **not built**, so nothing here has been validated by listening. That's the honest gap.\n","created_at":"2026-07-25T22:50:12Z","updated_at":"2026-08-16T13:52:54Z","closed_at":"2026-08-16T13:52:54Z","merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"c01a6a53a2a15ba50ee6897a28e2b134e56aa91d","assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"labels":[],"milestone":null,"draft":false,"commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flowercomputers/bogkit/pulls/5/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flowercomputers/bogkit/pulls/5/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flowercomputers/bogkit/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flowercomputers/bogkit/issues/5/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flowercomputers/bogkit/statuses/f41117c7876a12afdaf9b043cb618039a52838db","head":{"label":"dnbrwstr:tompkins-audio-search","ref":"tompkins-audio-search","sha":"f41117c7876a12afdaf9b043cb618039a52838db","user":{"login":"dnbrwstr","id":357216,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM1NzIxNg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/357216?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr","html_url":"https://github.com/dnbrwstr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dnbrwstr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.